{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1640 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "d0d9ec265ef0cc143f99cdcc6cac31532ceeb0315dc89e7402cd440b29dd7c45" :edb/db-before-t 1639 :edb/db-after-t 1640 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534952 50 #inst "2026-10-08T11:59:23.047Z" 13194139534952 true] [17592186119420 1100 "The set $\\{ \\mathbf{a_1},\\mathbf{a_2} \\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be $\\mathbf{a}_2$ if\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/assets/2fc772925dd6b793bdcb3a1eca96ea7e7f5fed17425f3500638340e047170c60.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/assets/f73258610652de9a656f3c4ed202241c58c98f18391b1393dabe62d92d9bef7d.png)" 13194139534952 true] [17592186119420 1100 "The set $\\{\\mathbf{a_1},\\mathbf{a_2} \\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be $\\mathbf{a}_2$ if\n$\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534952 false] [17592186119420 1145 "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx_1 = x_2 = 0\n$$\n\nOtherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$\nTo see why, notice that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$\nIn other words, the vectors $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ are linearly dependent, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3}a_{1} - a_{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 +x_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas the solution\n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac 1 3\n$$\n\nand $x_2=-1$, which is different from $x_1=x_2=0$. Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." 13194139534952 false] [17592186119420 1145 "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/assets/210a5c50467861cdfe48b46f2098212bbf387270b420a05394e318a0e0b48c74.png)\nTo see why, notice that \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/assets/91a29b57d32ed8c8e702f2988f7714ffc343ed6b4707b86b49b708ffad5c8bc8.png)\nIn other words, the vectors $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ are linearly dependent, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{a}_1  +x_2  \\mathbf{a}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas the solution $x_1=\\dfrac 1 3$ and $x_2=-1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." 13194139534952 true] [17592186119421 1100 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/assets/4af8a5ea61752a394d3c3313e5cbda76ad1981d8d62573d533ece0db0dd38820.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{a}_1 - \\mathbf{a}_2 -2 \\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3  = \\mathbf{0}$ only if $x_1=x_2=x_3 = 0.$\n\n- The set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is linearly independent." 13194139534952 true] [17592186119421 1100 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{a}_1 - \\mathbf{a}_2 -2 \\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}$.\n2. $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}$ only if $x_1=x_2=x_3 = 0$.\n3. The set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is linearly independent." 13194139534952 false] [17592186119421 1145 "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{v}_3= \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0\n$$\n\nOtherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n$a_{1} - a_{2} - 2a_{3}|= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} - 2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}4 + 0 - 4 \\\\ -1 - 1 + 2 \\\\ 0 + 0 - 14\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}; \\ne 0$\n- Statement II is true. The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n${4x_{1} + 2x_{3} = 0; - x_{1} + x_{2} - x_{3} = 0; 7x_{3} = 0$.\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n${-x_{1} + x_{2} - x_{3} = 0; 4x_{1} + 2x_{3} = 0; 7x_{3} = 0$\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0$.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534952 false] [17592186119421 1145 "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{v}_3= \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}-2{a}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4+0-4 \\\\ -1-1+2 \\\\ 0+0-14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534952 true] [17592186119422 1100 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/assets/8d88cae0e40e7a377314e359086dc45fa0460c17ed55fe379cc15537886b2623.png)\n\nAssume that $x_1,x_2,$ and $x_3$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n\n- The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent.\n\n- The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly dependent." 13194139534952 true] [17592186119422 1100 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nAssume that $x_1,x_2$, and $x_3$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n2. The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2$, and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent.\n3. The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2$, and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly dependent." 13194139534952 false] [17592186119422 1145 "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2$, and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3 \\mathbf{v}_3 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0\n$$\n\nOtherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation\n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{2x_{1} - x_{2} + 2x_{3} = 0; 4x_{1} + 4x_{2} + 5x_{3} = 0; 6x_{2} + x_{3} = 0\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-1)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are $x_1$ and $x_2$, while $x_3$ is the free variable.\n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, statements II is false, while statements I and III are true." 13194139534952 false] [17592186119422 1145 "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3 \\mathbf{v}_3 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are $x_1$ and $x_2,$ while $x_3$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements II is false, while statements I and III are true." 13194139534952 true] [17592186119844 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322161 13194139534952 true] [17592186119844 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322196 13194139534952 true] [17592186119844 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322217 13194139534952 true] [17592186119844 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322224 13194139534952 true] [17592186119845 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322168 13194139534952 true] [17592186119845 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322175 13194139534952 true] [17592186119845 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322182 13194139534952 true] [17592186119845 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322189 13194139534952 true] [17592186119846 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322203 13194139534952 true] [17592186119846 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322210 13194139534952 true] [17592186119846 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322231 13194139534952 true] [17592186119846 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322238 13194139534952 true] [17592186119846 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322245 13194139534952 true] [17592186120074 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534952 true] [17592186120074 1100 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-2{a}_{3}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent." 13194139534952 true] [17592186120074 1100 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{1} + a_{2} - 2a_{3} = 0$.\n2. $x_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} + x_{3}a_{3} = 0$ only if $x_{1} = x_{2} = x_{3} = 0$.\n3. The set ${a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}}$ is linearly independent." 13194139534952 false] [17592186120074 1145 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-2{a}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+1-2 \\\\ 2+0-2 \\\\ 0+2-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=1$ and ${x}_{3}=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534952 true] [17592186120254 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534952 true] [17592186120254 1100 "The set ${a_{1}, a_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be $a_{2}$ if\n\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139534952 false] [17592186120254 1100 "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139534952 true] [17592186120254 1145 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]=5[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=5{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{a}_{1}-5{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 5{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 5{a}_{1}+(-1){a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=5$ and ${x}_{2}=-1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." 13194139534952 true] [17592186322161 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322162 13194139534952 true] [17592186322161 1089 "q-105296" 13194139534952 true] [17592186322161 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534952 true] [17592186322161 1100 "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139534952 true] [17592186322161 1113 #uuid "29227877-bb5a-5e8d-a3d6-ef6fec4266a8" 13194139534952 true] [17592186322161 1145 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." 13194139534952 true] [17592186322162 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322163 13194139534952 true] [17592186322162 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322164 13194139534952 true] [17592186322162 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322165 13194139534952 true] [17592186322162 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322166 13194139534952 true] [17592186322162 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322167 13194139534952 true] [17592186322162 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534952 true] [17592186322162 1041 "selection" 13194139534952 true] [17592186322162 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322166 13194139534952 true] [17592186322162 1110 #uuid "96b2ec99-a23b-526e-b472-ba5af949b008" 13194139534952 true] [17592186322163 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534952 true] [17592186322163 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534952 true] [17592186322163 1111 #uuid "348be599-aa14-56e9-a0f5-522f07b90594" 13194139534952 true] [17592186322164 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534952 true] [17592186322164 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534952 true] [17592186322164 1111 #uuid "a6b8a7f5-341a-59f9-9b79-9ca8e823ff72" 13194139534952 true] [17592186322165 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534952 true] [17592186322165 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534952 true] [17592186322165 1111 #uuid "cebf84bf-2cab-5237-aa21-3119bc62546a" 13194139534952 true] [17592186322166 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534952 true] [17592186322166 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534952 true] [17592186322166 1111 #uuid "d13a9ee8-7991-5dcc-aded-17b229330d37" 13194139534952 true] [17592186322167 1034 "[\\begin{